<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sher Muhammad Khan of Malerkotla</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sher Muhammad Khan was nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malerkotla">Malerkotla</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in the early 18th century, serving under the Mughal governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirhind">Sirhind</a>. In 1705 he protested against the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zorawar-singh">Zorawar Singh</a> and Fateh Singh, the young sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a>, who were walled up alive at Sirhind. His plea, remembered as the 'haah da naara', was honoured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, who spared Malerkotla in later conflicts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sher-muhammad-khan-of-malerkotla.jpg" alt="Sher Muhammad Khan of Malerkotla" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reduced and drawn by S. Abdos Sobhan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_of_Malerkotla_State_from_a_map_of_British_and_native_states_in_the_Cis-Sutlej_Division_between_1847%E2%80%9351,_by_Abdos_Sobhan,_1858.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
